Below is a **complete, self‑contained Java program** that shows how `CompletableFuture` can be used to write clear, non‑blocking concurrent code.  
The example simulates two independent I/O‑bound services (`UserService` and `OrderService`) that run in parallel, then combines their results into a richer domain object (`UserProfile`). It also demonstrates:

* how to start asynchronous tasks (`supplyAsync`);
* chaining (`thenApply`, `thenCombine`);
* error handling (`exceptionally`, `handle`);
* time‑outs (`orTimeout`);
* consuming the result on a different thread (`thenAcceptAsync`);
* waiting for several futures to finish (`allOf`);
* graceful shutdown of the executor.

```java
// File: CompletableFutureDemo.java
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

/** Simple domain objects */
record User(String id, String name, String email) { }
record Order(String id, String userId, double amount) { }
record UserProfile(User user, List<Order> orders) { }

/** Simulated remote service that returns a User after a delay */
class UserService {
    private final Random rnd = new Random();

    CompletableFuture<User> getUserAsync(String userId, Executor executor) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            simulateDelay(1_000); // 1 sec
            if (rnd.nextInt(10) == 0)   // 10% chance of failure
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to fetch user " + userId);
            return new User(userId, "Alice" + userId, "alice"+userId+"@example.com");
        }, executor);
    }
}

/** Simulated remote service that returns a list of Orders after a delay */
class OrderService {
    private final Random rnd = new Random();

    CompletableFuture<List<Order>> getOrdersAsync(String userId, Executor executor) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
            simulateDelay(1_500); // 1.5 sec
            if (rnd.nextInt(20) == 0)  // 5% chance of failure
                throw new RuntimeException("Failed to fetch orders for user " + userId);
            // Generate a random number of orders
            int count = rnd.nextInt(5) + 1;
            return IntStream.range(0, count)
                    .mapToObj(i -> new Order("ORD-" + i, userId, rnd.nextDouble() * 200))
                    .collect(Collectors.toList());
        }, executor);
    }
}

/** Utility method to pause the current thread for a bit */
private static void simulateDelay(long millis) {
    try {
        Thread.sleep(millis);
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
    }
}

/** Main demo class */
public class CompletableFutureDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Create a bounded thread pool that will be used by all async tasks
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(
                Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(),
                new ThreadFactory() {
                    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
                    @Override
                    public Thread newThread(Runnable r) {
                        return new Thread(r, "async-pool-" + count.incrementAndGet());
                    }
                });

        // Instantiate services
        UserService userService = new UserService();
        OrderService orderService = new OrderService();

        String userId = "42";

        // Kick off both remote calls concurrently
        CompletableFuture<User> userFuture = userService.getUserAsync(userId, executor)
                .orTimeout(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)      // fail fast if taking > 2s
                .exceptionally(ex -> {
                    System.err.println("User fetch failed: " + ex.getMessage());
                    // Provide a default user so that we can still fetch orders
                    return new User(userId, "Unknown", "unknown@example.com");
                });

        CompletableFuture<List<Order>> ordersFuture = orderService.getOrdersAsync(userId, executor)
                .orTimeout(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
                .exceptionally(ex -> {
                    System.err.println("Orders fetch failed: " + ex.getMessage());
                    // Fallback to an empty order list
                    return List.of();
                });

        // Combine the two results once both are ready
        CompletableFuture<UserProfile> profileFuture = userFuture.thenCombine(
                ordersFuture,
                UserProfile::new
        );

        // Process the profile on a different thread (e.g., UI thread, logger, etc.)
        profileFuture.thenAcceptAsync(profile -> {
            System.out.println("\n=== User Profile ===");
            System.out.println("User: " + profile.user().name() + " (" + profile.user().email() + ")");
            System.out.println("Orders:");
            profile.orders().forEach(order ->
                    System.out.printf("  - %s: $%.2f%n", order.id(), order.amount()));
        }, executor);

        // Example of waiting for multiple independent futures to finish
        CompletableFuture<Void> allDone = CompletableFuture.allOf(userFuture, ordersFuture, profileFuture);
        try {
            allDone.get(); // blocks until all are finished
        } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        // Clean up
        executor.shutdown();
        try {
            if (!executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                executor.shutdownNow();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            executor.shutdownNow();
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        System.out.println("\nDemo finished.");
    }
}
```

### What the code does

1. **Parallel I/O** – `userFuture` and `ordersFuture` start simultaneously using the same thread pool.  
2. **Timeout & Fallback** – Each future uses `orTimeout` to abort if it takes too long, and `exceptionally` to supply a sensible default when something goes wrong.  
3. **Combining** – `thenCombine` merges the results into a `UserProfile`.  
4. **Non‑blocking consumption** – `thenAcceptAsync` prints the profile on the same executor thread (you could replace this with any UI or logging thread).  
5. **Wait for all** – `CompletableFuture.allOf` allows the main thread to block until the whole pipeline has finished, demonstrating how to coordinate multiple futures.  
6. **Graceful shutdown** – The executor is shut down cleanly after all tasks finish.

Feel free to replace the simulated services with real I/O (HTTP calls, database queries, etc.) and adapt the time‑outs and exception handling to your production needs. Happy concurrent coding!
